Subject: [PATCH] Make the latency policy opt-in and reloadable without a logout

Revision 21 compiled the policy on behind the baked kernel release, so
installing the package was enabling the experiment. Invert that for public
testers: add a master switch that defaults off, and move the four policy
knobs out of load-time statics so a running compositor can be re-policied.

The exact-kernel gate keeps its load-time static form but changes role. It
is no longer the enable, it is the outer AND condition, so a tester on a
different kernel still fails closed to stock and cannot switch the
experiment on from a config file.

Policy now lives in a file-local object of relaxed atomics holding the
already-combined effective values, so no frame path locks, allocates or
recombines the gate with a knob. The only writer is a KConfigWatcher
callback on the main thread, wired once from the first RenderLoop
construction against kwinrc:

  [LatencyExperiment]
  Enabled=true                # master switch, default off
  SchedulerMarginMode=2       # 0 fixed, 1 shadow, 2 adaptive
  RenderBudgetMode=2          # 0 stock, 1 shadow, 2 adaptive
  VrrRepaintCoalescing=true
  SchedulerMarginUs=1000      # clamped to 0..10000

An absent key falls back to the environment variable that used to own it
(KWIN_LATENCY_EXPERIMENT, KWIN_ADAPTIVE_COMPOSITOR_MARGIN,
KWIN_ADAPTIVE_RENDER_BUDGET, KWIN_VRR_REPAINT_COALESCING,
KWIN_COMPOSITOR_SCHEDULING_MARGIN_US), so every documented variable keeps
working as the boot-time default. KConfigWatcher reloads the file before
it emits, so

  kwriteconfig6 --file kwinrc --group LatencyExperiment --key Enabled true --notify

takes effect in the live session. That is what a per-game Steam wrapper
needs, and it is also the recovery path for a tester whose session went
wrong: the compositor is exactly what they cannot restart.

Two consequences worth stating. The PreciseTimer wakeups from patches
0001 and 0002 are merged-upstream backports, not experimental policy, so
compositeTimer construction stays gated on the kernel release alone;
"disabled" means stock policy values driven onto the precise timer, not
stock code paths. And delayedVrrTimer is now built whenever the gate
passes rather than when coalescing happens to be on at construction,
because a timer that does not exist cannot be armed when the policy flips
on; both VRR timers are disarmed on every arm and stop so a flip mid-frame
cannot strand a pending wakeup on the timer that armed it.

Capture and telemetry statics (KWIN_LATENCY_CAPTURE,
KWIN_LOG_PERFORMANCE_DATA, the sample divisor) stay environment-only
load-time statics; they select what is measured, not what is applied.
